WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.177 --> 00:00:01.436
("The Best" by Tina Turner)
- Alrighty,

00:00:01.436 --> 00:00:02.168
ladies and gentlemen,

00:00:02.168 --> 00:00:04.504
boys and girls, we're here.

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:06.089
If you know who's 
about to come out stage,

00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:07.044
I know you're excited.

00:00:07.044 --> 00:00:08.985
He's one of the hottest speakers

00:00:08.985 --> 00:00:10.428
on the planet right now.

00:00:10.428 --> 00:00:11.566
Just like what we do here,

00:00:11.566 --> 00:00:13.417
this man builds businesses.

00:00:13.417 --> 00:00:15.896
Fresh out of college, 
took his family wine

00:00:15.896 --> 00:00:17.704
business from three million

00:00:17.704 --> 00:00:21.605
to 60 million, in less
than four or five years.

00:00:21.605 --> 00:00:23.230
Along the way, 
he's become an incredibly

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:26.298
successful venture
capitalist, an angel investor,

00:00:26.298 --> 00:00:27.844
invested in a couple 
of small companies

00:00:27.844 --> 00:00:28.733
you may have heard of,

00:00:28.733 --> 00:00:31.888
called Facebook 
and Twitter, and Uber.

00:00:31.888 --> 00:00:33.330
He's got a brand new book coming out

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:35.890
very soon, it's called #AskGaryVee,

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:37.881
because he is Gary Vee.

00:00:37.881 --> 00:00:39.060
Ladies and gentlemen, please be

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.766
on your feet and welcome to the stage,

00:00:40.766 --> 00:00:43.083
the last speaker for ICON 2016,

00:00:43.083 --> 00:00:46.130
give it up for Gary Vaynerchuk.

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:47.070
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.351
Gary Vee!

00:00:48.351 --> 00:00:49.671
(energetic rap music)

00:00:49.671 --> 00:00:53.380
Gary Vee!

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:54.812
(laughs)

00:00:57.386 --> 00:00:58.739
(speaker is drowned out by loud music)

00:00:58.739 --> 00:01:00.609
- Thank you so much.

00:01:05.021 --> 00:01:06.605
Thank you!

00:01:10.074 --> 00:01:11.487
Thank you!

00:01:11.487 --> 00:01:13.851
Thank you so much!

00:01:15.765 --> 00:01:16.559
So, (audience member cheers).

00:01:16.559 --> 00:01:17.595
Thank you!

00:01:17.595 --> 00:01:18.631
How's everybody?

00:01:18.631 --> 00:01:19.749
(audience cheers)

00:01:19.749 --> 00:01:21.415
Awesome.

00:01:21.415 --> 00:01:22.817
Couple things,

00:01:22.817 --> 00:01:25.560
first, I was asked very politely

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.294
not to curse, so they'll
be no cursing. (laughter)

00:01:28.294 --> 00:01:30.021
(audience boos)

00:01:30.021 --> 00:01:32.479
It's just the truth.

00:01:32.479 --> 00:01:34.003
Second of all, because I wanna figure out

00:01:34.003 --> 00:01:35.242
where I wanna take this talk,

00:01:35.242 --> 00:01:38.087
based on the crowd,

00:01:38.087 --> 00:01:40.159
how many people in this room

00:01:40.159 --> 00:01:41.683
don't know who I am and don't have

00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:43.003
a good feel for my spiel?

00:01:43.003 --> 00:01:45.229
Please raise your hand.

00:01:45.229 --> 00:01:48.927
Alright, that hurts. (laughter)

00:01:48.927 --> 00:01:49.597
Makes sense.

00:01:49.597 --> 00:01:51.092
Alright, so I'm gonna go very quickly,

00:01:51.092 --> 00:01:53.124
since it's not that big of a percentage,

00:01:53.124 --> 00:01:55.298
then I'm gonna go into my spiel,

00:01:55.298 --> 00:01:57.899
and then I honestly wanna go into Q and A,

00:01:57.899 --> 00:02:00.174
as quickly as possible, because I think

00:02:00.174 --> 00:02:02.125
70% of you have heard a lot of this,

00:02:02.125 --> 00:02:04.011
the 30% of you are gonna enjoy it,

00:02:04.011 --> 00:02:05.596
but I think if we can go into

00:02:05.596 --> 00:02:07.933
the context and the details,

00:02:07.933 --> 00:02:09.036
it'll be far greater.

00:02:09.036 --> 00:02:11.955
So, actually, I'm gonna go
with a very short version

00:02:11.955 --> 00:02:14.129
of it, 'cause there
wasn't that many hands.

00:02:14.129 --> 00:02:15.917
I was born in the Soviet Union,

00:02:15.917 --> 00:02:17.380
so I grew up an immigrant.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:19.229
What that means is,

00:02:19.229 --> 00:02:20.947
how many people here
were not born in America?

00:02:20.947 --> 00:02:22.218
Raise your hand.

00:02:22.218 --> 00:02:24.270
Wow, let's clap it up for the immigrants

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:25.103
in the house.

00:02:25.103 --> 00:02:25.875
(audience cheers)

00:02:25.875 --> 00:02:26.638
I like that.

00:02:26.638 --> 00:02:28.061
Oh actually this is very international,

00:02:28.061 --> 00:02:29.036
you flew in, you don't live here,

00:02:29.036 --> 00:02:30.418
got it, sorry, I screwed up.

00:02:30.418 --> 00:02:34.298
Okay, so, I grew up very poor,

00:02:34.298 --> 00:02:37.143
I lived in a studio apartment
with eight family members,

00:02:37.143 --> 00:02:38.565
so I've got grit, right?

00:02:38.565 --> 00:02:39.784
Like, I'm not scared to work,

00:02:39.784 --> 00:02:40.901
I think one of the biggest problems

00:02:40.901 --> 00:02:42.466
right now, is we're living through

00:02:42.466 --> 00:02:45.556
one of the great generations
of fake entrepreneurship,

00:02:45.556 --> 00:02:46.814
because so many people are able

00:02:46.814 --> 00:02:49.376
to raise money, and so you've got,

00:02:49.376 --> 00:02:50.741
you just have people 
that are not supposed

00:02:50.741 --> 00:02:52.491
to be running businesses,
running businesses,

00:02:52.491 --> 00:02:53.789
'cause there was so much capital

00:02:53.789 --> 00:02:55.141
for the last seven or eight years,

00:02:55.141 --> 00:02:57.396
and that's interesting for me to watch,

00:02:57.396 --> 00:02:59.286
though the stuff is
starting to hit the fan,

00:02:59.286 --> 00:03:01.541
so, that will play itself out.

00:03:01.541 --> 00:03:04.365
I was a hustler, I know
stuff, I know, (laughter)

00:03:04.365 --> 00:03:07.331
I was a hustler from the get,

00:03:07.331 --> 00:03:10.623
I was lemonade stand, baseball cards,

00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:14.828
all that stuff, so I was
really a salesperson,

00:03:14.828 --> 00:03:16.860
through and through.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:18.639
When I was 12, I was making two

00:03:18.639 --> 00:03:21.840
to $3,000 a weekend,
selling baseball cards

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.669
in the malls of New Jersey,

00:03:23.669 --> 00:03:24.655
and I don't know about you guys,

00:03:24.655 --> 00:03:29.187
but when you have $30,000 in cash,

00:03:29.187 --> 00:03:33.007
under your bed, and you're 14 years old,

00:03:33.007 --> 00:03:34.836
and you're not selling weed,

00:03:34.836 --> 00:03:35.953
you're doing a good job.

00:03:35.953 --> 00:03:37.204
(laughter and applause)

00:03:37.204 --> 00:03:40.623
So, that was me.

00:03:41.897 --> 00:03:43.624
My dad dragged me into the liquor store

00:03:43.624 --> 00:03:47.232
that he now owned, he was
making two bucks an hour

00:03:47.232 --> 00:03:48.471
as a stockboy, when he first came

00:03:48.471 --> 00:03:49.974
to America, so you can imagine

00:03:49.974 --> 00:03:54.322
how much my parents are
very much my heroes.

00:03:54.322 --> 00:03:56.557
He owned a small store,
really not much bigger

00:03:56.557 --> 00:03:58.471
than the stage I'm on, it
was a 400 square foot store,

00:03:58.471 --> 00:04:00.189
then he built it up, made it bigger,

00:04:00.189 --> 00:04:02.302
I was dragged into it. 
I hated it.

00:04:02.302 --> 00:04:04.131
It was not fun for me,

00:04:04.131 --> 00:04:07.565
I'd bagged ice for 15 hours a day,

00:04:07.565 --> 00:04:08.702
I was in the basement.

00:04:08.702 --> 00:04:09.617
How many people here have seen

00:04:09.617 --> 00:04:11.384
the movie Goonies?

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:12.657
Raise your hand. (audience cheers)

00:04:12.657 --> 00:04:14.741
I literally like was Sloth

00:04:14.741 --> 00:04:16.265
for like three years. (laughter)

00:04:16.265 --> 00:04:18.398
I was basically chained to the basement

00:04:18.398 --> 00:04:21.812
of my dad's liquor store. It sucked.

00:04:21.812 --> 00:04:23.336
Finally I was allowed upstairs

00:04:23.336 --> 00:04:26.476
and I realized that people collected wine

00:04:26.476 --> 00:04:28.163
and that was my connection to the world

00:04:28.163 --> 00:04:30.235
I came from, with baseball cards

00:04:30.235 --> 00:04:32.632
and things of that
nature, and that started

00:04:32.632 --> 00:04:36.472
kind of my interest
into my dad's business.

00:04:36.472 --> 00:04:38.707
'Cause at that point I was
a punk entrepreneur kid,

00:04:38.707 --> 00:04:40.965
I thought I could run the
business better than my dad,

00:04:40.965 --> 00:04:43.464
but I wasn't interested in
selling Coors Light and Absolut,

00:04:43.464 --> 00:04:45.110
but this wine thing was interesting,

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:46.491
and it felt like the marketing thing

00:04:46.491 --> 00:04:48.543
that I couldn't define at that point,

00:04:48.543 --> 00:04:50.569
storytelling, bullcrapping,
whatever you want to call it,

00:04:50.569 --> 00:04:52.745
like, I couldn't figure out what I was,

00:04:52.745 --> 00:04:54.330
but I knew I was somethin',

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.077
and I knew that it wasn't interesting

00:04:56.077 --> 00:04:57.549
to deploy that against Coors Light

00:04:57.549 --> 00:04:58.683
or an Absolut, but the wine thing,

00:04:58.683 --> 00:05:00.227
most people didn't understand,

00:05:00.227 --> 00:05:02.157
there was an opportunity to really market.

00:05:02.157 --> 00:05:04.839
So I, from 17 to 18 years old,

00:05:04.839 --> 00:05:07.297
16 to 18 years old, I
probably learned more

00:05:07.297 --> 00:05:09.492
about wine than most
people should ever learn

00:05:09.492 --> 00:05:12.620
in their lives, I was a terrible student,

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:15.201
I started getting F's in fourth grade,

00:05:15.201 --> 00:05:18.228
I punted it very early in my life.

00:05:18.228 --> 00:05:20.463
I would tell you that I
if could wish anything

00:05:20.463 --> 00:05:22.556
for everybody in this
conference right now,

00:05:22.556 --> 00:05:24.100
if I could actually wish you anything

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:26.050
besides health, that would be number one,

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:28.712
but if I could wish you
anything besides health,

00:05:28.712 --> 00:05:30.865
I would wish you self-awareness.

00:05:30.865 --> 00:05:34.096
I truly believe that
self-awareness (audience applauds),

00:05:34.096 --> 00:05:36.534
I truly believe that
self-awareness is a big deal

00:05:36.534 --> 00:05:39.623
and my self-awareness
was extreme as a kid,

00:05:39.623 --> 00:05:41.575
and I just knew who I was,

00:05:41.575 --> 00:05:42.853
and even though the market.

00:05:42.853 --> 00:05:44.945
Don't forget, I was an immigrant,

00:05:44.945 --> 00:05:46.408
so the way out for immigrants

00:05:46.408 --> 00:05:48.706
in America, for a lot of you
that don't know, is education,

00:05:48.706 --> 00:05:49.835
they push it hard.
(audience member shouts)

00:05:49.835 --> 00:05:52.273
So all my friends, my parents' friends

00:05:52.273 --> 00:05:54.589
were pushing education,
all the other Russian kids

00:05:54.589 --> 00:05:56.885
went to Yale, and if
they were like a loser

00:05:56.885 --> 00:05:58.287
they went to NYU. (laughter)

00:05:58.287 --> 00:06:01.477
I was like getting F's, but my parents

00:06:01.477 --> 00:06:03.305
supported my entrepreneurship,

00:06:03.305 --> 00:06:04.402
but more importantly than that,

00:06:04.402 --> 00:06:06.007
I was gifted, 'cause I really,

00:06:06.007 --> 00:06:08.100
I'm trying to figure out how to teach it

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.477
or elevate it, because it's a big deal,

00:06:10.477 --> 00:06:11.900
but I was gifted with self-awareness

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:15.597
by 12 or 13, even though
all my friends' parents

00:06:15.597 --> 00:06:18.828
told me I was a loser, 'cause
I was failling classes,

00:06:18.828 --> 00:06:21.591
even though my teachers said that,

00:06:21.591 --> 00:06:23.948
I knew, I knew because
I was making a crapload

00:06:23.948 --> 00:06:26.519
of cash every weekend (laughter).

00:06:26.519 --> 00:06:28.490
But I also knew because
I just could taste it,

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:29.770
I knew who I was.

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:31.213
So, anyway, I got involved

00:06:31.213 --> 00:06:33.610
in my dad's liquor store,

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:35.764
my plan was not to go to college,

00:06:35.764 --> 00:06:37.389
my mom, this is how much my mom

00:06:37.389 --> 00:06:39.116
didn't know about the education system

00:06:39.116 --> 00:06:40.957
in America. My mom came to me

00:06:40.957 --> 00:06:45.419
in February of my graduating
year of high school.

00:06:45.419 --> 00:06:46.990
You know, you're graduating in 90 days

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:49.449
and goes to me, "So what
are we doing about college?"

00:06:49.449 --> 00:06:51.501
I'm like, I've been doing
a lot of things mom,

00:06:51.501 --> 00:06:53.675
I'm not going, and then
she punched me in the face

00:06:53.675 --> 00:06:56.072
and (laughter), like a
couple of days later I,

00:06:56.072 --> 00:06:58.063
this is not a joke, this
is how I went to college,

00:06:58.063 --> 00:07:00.136
a couple days later, I got a postcard

00:07:00.136 --> 00:07:02.432
in the mail from Mount Ida College,

00:07:02.432 --> 00:07:04.321
and I filled out my name and address

00:07:04.321 --> 00:07:06.933
and send it in and that's
how I went to college.

00:07:06.933 --> 00:07:08.965
So, that fall I went to Mount Ida College

00:07:08.965 --> 00:07:11.069
in Newton, Mass, I was in my dorm room

00:07:11.069 --> 00:07:14.340
on a very faithful day,
playing Madden '95,

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:17.367
dominating by the way, (laughter)

00:07:17.367 --> 00:07:19.318
my boy comes in (chuckles), and says,

00:07:19.318 --> 00:07:20.537
"Hey, you gotta come see this,"

00:07:20.537 --> 00:07:21.756
I went into a dorm room,

00:07:21.756 --> 00:07:22.975
there's eight dudes,

00:07:22.975 --> 00:07:24.519
they're standing over a computer,

00:07:24.519 --> 00:07:27.038
I had a word processor for the papers

00:07:27.038 --> 00:07:28.379
I was supposed to do.

00:07:28.379 --> 00:07:31.264
So I, at this point, I'm 18 years old,

00:07:31.264 --> 00:07:34.231
I've spent maybe 30
minutes on the Internet,

00:07:34.231 --> 00:07:38.396
excuse me, I'm sorry, 30 minutes
on a computer in my life.

00:07:38.396 --> 00:07:40.834
I'm old, so like that's
how we did it, right?

00:07:40.834 --> 00:07:43.191
And so, I'm standing in
front of the computer,

00:07:43.191 --> 00:07:47.254
I understand, at this point
the information super highways

00:07:47.254 --> 00:07:50.200
in the news, I hear for the first time

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:52.455
(loud mouth beeping),

00:07:52.455 --> 00:07:55.645
is 1994, (laughter) I'm like,

00:07:55.645 --> 00:07:56.560
is this the Internet?

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:59.018
The information super highway, oh my god!

00:07:59.018 --> 00:08:03.655
I, eight dudes are standing
over an AOL chatroom

00:08:03.655 --> 00:08:07.669
for five hours, I waited three hours

00:08:07.669 --> 00:08:09.559
for my turn, (laughter)

00:08:09.559 --> 00:08:12.627
I get on the Internet,
and within seven minutes,

00:08:12.627 --> 00:08:13.877
and I could barely use a computer,

00:08:13.877 --> 00:08:18.225
I still can't type,
like, in seven minutes,

00:08:18.225 --> 00:08:21.171
I'm on a random AOL message board

00:08:21.171 --> 00:08:24.016
where people are selling
and trading baseball cards.

00:08:24.016 --> 00:08:25.946
And within 30 minutes of ever being

00:08:25.946 --> 00:08:27.592
on the Internet in 1994,

00:08:27.592 --> 00:08:30.599
I realized, holy crap, I don't need

00:08:30.599 --> 00:08:33.707
to open up 4,000 liquor stores,

00:08:33.707 --> 00:08:36.206
I'm gonna sell on this thing.

00:08:36.206 --> 00:08:37.344
And that's my relationship

00:08:37.344 --> 00:08:38.400
with the Internet. (audience applauds)

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.168
Don't clap, 'cause I didnt' accomplish

00:08:40.168 --> 00:08:43.674
anything yet, but here
it comes. (laughter)

00:08:43.674 --> 00:08:47.067
I figured out, I started
getting super interested,

00:08:47.067 --> 00:08:48.205
I, like, you know?

00:08:48.205 --> 00:08:50.094
When I came back from, we
didn't have cell phones,

00:08:50.094 --> 00:08:51.882
and you didn't know your
friend's phone numbers

00:08:51.882 --> 00:08:53.641
back then, like when I
came back from school

00:08:53.641 --> 00:08:54.900
that summer, I found a couple nerds

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:56.255
from high school, I was like, yo, nerds,

00:08:56.255 --> 00:08:57.210
you know this crap, right?

00:08:57.210 --> 00:08:58.571
They're like, "Yeah," I'm like, alright,

00:08:58.571 --> 00:09:00.179
like, I went to their
house, they got like a modem

00:09:00.179 --> 00:09:01.705
in their phone, they're weird as hell,

00:09:01.705 --> 00:09:03.208
it's in the basement, it's awkward,

00:09:03.208 --> 00:09:05.017
but I'm like, this is it.

00:09:05.017 --> 00:09:07.506
And so (laughter), two years later,

00:09:07.506 --> 00:09:11.062
I launch the second
e-commerce wine business

00:09:11.062 --> 00:09:13.459
in America, called winelibrary.com.

00:09:13.459 --> 00:09:15.857
And so, it's comin', (laughter)

00:09:15.857 --> 00:09:19.006
and then in 1998, actually
this is a good story,

00:09:19.006 --> 00:09:20.311
this will, you know what?

00:09:20.311 --> 00:09:21.428
This is a good story for a lot of people

00:09:21.428 --> 00:09:23.196
in this room, you know, I've audited

00:09:23.196 --> 00:09:24.496
a lot of people in this room,

00:09:24.496 --> 00:09:26.206
looking at people using the hashtags,

00:09:26.206 --> 00:09:27.669
I've spoke here three or four years ago,

00:09:27.669 --> 00:09:29.924
I've got a solid sense of
what's going on in here.

00:09:29.924 --> 00:09:31.935
I'm really excited
because this little story

00:09:31.935 --> 00:09:33.804
I'm about to tell, actually,

00:09:33.804 --> 00:09:36.296
I've never said it this way,
Joe Mama, I see you brother.

00:09:36.296 --> 00:09:37.839
A lot of people have heard this before,

00:09:37.839 --> 00:09:39.647
I'm actually gonna tie this in nicely.

00:09:39.647 --> 00:09:41.720
I would tell you the most
interesting thing for me,

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:43.528
in this room today, is the following,

00:09:43.528 --> 00:09:46.210
I believe that I'm standing in a room

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:49.135
of non-marketers or brand people.

00:09:49.135 --> 00:09:50.172
Non!

00:09:50.172 --> 00:09:51.919
I think I'm sitting in the room

00:09:51.919 --> 00:09:55.721
of transactional salespeople,

00:09:55.721 --> 00:09:58.150
and by the way, I like that,

00:09:58.150 --> 00:09:58.841
just so everybody knows,

00:09:58.841 --> 00:10:00.995
I'm not saying that's bad or good,

00:10:00.995 --> 00:10:02.823
but I feel that the far majority

00:10:02.823 --> 00:10:07.142
